            <ArticleTitle>Sleeve gastrectomy drives persistent shifts in the gut microbiome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>916-924</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1550-7289(17)30003-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.soard.2017.01.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Changes in the gut microbiome following bariatric surgery have been causally linked to metabolic benefits.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We sought to characterize and assess the stability of gut microbiome shifts following sleeve gastrectomy (SG).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University laboratories.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Diet-induced obese mice were randomized to SG or sham surgery. Mice were housed individually or cohoused such that one SG mouse was housed with one weight-matched, sham-operated mouse. Fecal samples were collected before and on postoperative days 7 and 28. Bacterial composition in feces was characterized by using next-generation Illumina sequencing of 16 S rRNA.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">SG mice lost more weight and were more insulin sensitive than sham mice independent of housing status (P&lt;.05). One week following surgery, fecal samples from all mice showed shifts in the microbiome that only persisted in SG-operated mice. Cohousing did not alter the microbial composition of SG-operated mice. Cohoused sham-operated mice showed a unique shift in microbial composition on postoperative day 28 that differed from individually housed, sham-operated mice (P&lt;.001). Cohousing did not affect metabolic outcomes of either SG or sham surgeries.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">SG results in acute and sustained shifts in the gut microbiome. SG associated shifts are not altered by reexposure to obesity-associated gut microbiota.</AbstractText>
